Feature: Convert Spark SQL notebook to PySpark with functions and variables fidelity

  As a data engineer, I need to convert the provided Spark.SQL() code and all supporting logic in "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" to idiomatic PySpark, including implementing or mapping all referenced reusable functions and constants, and ensuring that variable, function, parameter, and output handling is preserved according to the notebook's requirements and business logic.

  Background:
    Given the full content of the "fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb" notebook is available
    And the complete source code of all referenced reusable functions in DA_CCG_RSD_EU/ReusableFunctions is provided
    And the complete definition of all constants in DA_CCG_RSD_EU/Constants is provided
    And all required table and Unity Catalog/variable values (raw_unity_catalog, raw_unity_catalog_hist, edp_lkp_unity_catalog, dims_unity_catalog, config_unity_catalog, cl_control_table, etc.) are accessible
    And the functional requirements and output contract (output DataFrame/table/view, write mode, locations, formats, partitioning) are fully specified

  Scenario: Successful conversion of notebook to PySpark with function mapping (happy path)
    Given all variable widgets (target_table_path, partition_key, table_format, compression, table_name, unity_catalog, etc.) have proper values and types as string
    And required functions like "read_control_table" and "get_basejob_url" are available as PySpark functions
    And referenced tables and views exist in the specified Unity Catalogs
    When the Spark SQL code block is translated to equivalent PySpark DataFrame transformations using PySpark API (e.g., DataFrame.withColumn, join, filter, select, Window functions, etc.)
    And reusable functions are invoked with correct parameter passing semantics
    And variables/constants are referenced using their PySpark equivalents
    Then the resulting PySpark code loads, transforms, and produces the same logical result as the original Spark SQL query
    And the output DataFrame is written or exposed as per the specified output contract (e.g., saved to Delta/Parquet, registered as table or view)
    And performance optimizations for large-scale PySpark jobs are considered (e.g., broadcast joins for small dimension tables, repartitioning, SQLConfig inheritance)

  Scenario: Missing or incorrect reusable function source (error path)
    Given at least one referenced function such as "read_control_table" does not have a visible implementation
    When attempting to convert the SQL logic that requires this function
    Then an error message is raised with "Source code for function 'read_control_table' not provided. Unable to complete PySpark conversion."
    And the conversion logs the missing function and affected logic section(s)

  Scenario: Missing variable or constant definition (error path)
    Given a referenced constant such as "cl_control_table" is not defined in any loaded module
    When PySpark code translation attempts to interpolate/use this variable
    Then an error message is raised indicating "Constant 'cl_control_table' not defined. Please provide its definition."
    And all dependent logic on this constant is flagged as incomplete in the report

  Scenario: Incomplete SQL logic in notebook (error path)
    Given the attached notebook contains only partial SQL code, clipped or missing the ending CTEs/SELECT
    When attempting to define the full PySpark DataFrame transformation pipeline
    Then a blocking error message is returned: "Full SQL logic required. Portion after 'P05 AS (...)' is missing."
    And the output conversion is halted with a dependency request for the missing logic

  Scenario: Output contract not defined (error path)
    Given all code and functions are present
    And the PySpark transformation produces the final DataFrame
    But it is not specified whether to write as Delta, Parquet, table, or view, nor the save path/mode/partitioning
    When the conversion reaches the output writing stage
    Then an error is raised: "Output format and target not specified. Please provide write format, partition key(s), target path or table name, and write mode."
    And the conversion logs all missing output parameters for correction

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven test of widget variable ingestion and PySpark parameter resolution
    Given all notebook widget variables are set
    When the widgets are read from dbutils.widgets.get("<variable>")
    Then the resulting PySpark variables are assigned as string and used in proper locations
    And if a widget <widget_name> is missing, an exception is thrown: "Widget value for '<widget_name>' is required but was not provided."
    Examples:
      | widget_name                |
      | target_table_path          |
      | partition_key              |
      | table_format               |
      | compression                |
      | table_name                 |
      | unity_catalog              |
      | environment                |
      | project                    |
      | load_type                  |
      | view_unity_catalog_name    |
      | raw_unity_catalog          |
      | raw_unity_catalog_hist     |
      | config_unity_catalog       |
      | edp_lkp_unity_catalog      |
      | dims_unity_catalog         |

  Scenario Outline: Validation of parameter types, data formats, and reference integrity
    Given parameter <param_name> (from notebook widget or constant) is available
    When the value is read into the PySpark workflow
    Then it must be of type <expected_type>
    And conform to format <format_description>
    And if not, an error of type <error_type> is raised with message "<error_msg>"
    Examples:
      | param_name          | expected_type | format_description             | error_type   | error_msg                                        |
      | table_name          | string        | non-empty, valid table         | ValueError   | Table name widget is empty or not a string.      |
      | unity_catalog       | string        | Must be a valid UC catalog     | ValueError   | Unity catalog missing or of invalid format.      |
      | partition_key       | string        | Valid column in output schema  | KeyError     | Partition key not present in DataFrame columns.  |
      | environment         | string        | One of [dev, qa, prod]         | ValueError   | Invalid environment specified.                   |

  Scenario: SQL-specific logic is mapped to PySpark with 100% feature parity 
    Given all SQL CTEs, filters, joins, and window specifications are present in the provided code
    When the translation to PySpark is performed
    Then all calculated columns, join conditions, window functions (ROW_NUMBER OVER PARTITION BY ...), conditional expressions (CASE, COALESCE, IS NULL, etc.), filters, and aggregations are translated using official PySpark DataFrame APIs
    And the output schema's column names and data types match the original SQL result faithfully
    And all business logic (see: exclusion filters, COALESCE, data mappings, lookups, constants, CASE logic for div_cd, etc.) are implemented as per the SQL logic

  Scenario: Explicit error message when required business logic or additional domain rules are omitted
    Given the notebook or requirements reference business or domain-specific logic not visible in SQL
    When the conversion is attempted without these rules
    Then an error message is raised specifying the missing rule and where it is needed in the pipeline
    And documentation is updated to request exact business logic definition

  Scenario: Success criteria for comprehensive validation
    Given the PySpark conversion is completed
    When end-to-end tests are executed on representative sample input data
    Then the PySpark job output matches row count, column names, data types, value correctness, and functional side-effects (e.g., written data location, view registration) of the original Spark SQL implementation
    And data lineage can be traced from source to target via code and data mapping documentation

  Scenario: Data value and filter validation for exclusion and transformation logic
    Given explicit SQL filters or exclusions such as
      | dgsdt_cd.gl_segment_valueset_code = 'Bal Entity FS_CCG_COA' |
      | YEAR(dasaic.invoice_accounting_date)  >= YEAR(current_timestamp)-3 |
      | dasaic.invoice_source_code  NOT IN ('Receivables') |
      | supplier_number NOT IN (00032238,00032239,00080463,90000147) |
    When these are ported to PySpark
    Then equivalent DataFrame filter logic uses the same values and semantics
    And NOT IN/exclusion lists are preserved as literal lists in the PySpark filter
    And date calculations and expressions use PySpark functions (e.g., year(), current_timestamp()) as per Spark SQL logic

  Scenario: All referenced lookups and joins present and using required lookup tables
    Given SQL joins reference lookup tables (edp_lkp_unity_catalog, dims_unity_catalog, raw_unity_catalog, etc.)
    When joining in PySpark code
    Then left joins (`DataFrame.join(..., how='left')`) are used with exact key fields and join conditions as per the SQL code
    And join keys and aliases match SQL intent (e.g., using COALESCE as per the SQL logic)
    And any additional lookup filtering (e.g., lkup_typ_nm='TFS_CC_TO_DIV', upper(lkup_key_01)='USORAFIN') is preserved

  Scenario Outline: Explicit error when referenced tables or columns are missing in the target data source
    Given input table <table_ref> is referenced in the SQL logic
    When trying to read this table into a Spark DataFrame
    Then an error "Table '<table_ref>' does not exist or cannot be loaded." is raised
    Examples:
      | table_ref                                            |
      | {raw_unity_catalog}.dw_ap_sla_aging_invoice_ca       |
      | {dims_unity_catalog}.dim_wf_company                  |
      | {edp_lkp_unity_catalog}.edp_lkup                     |

  Scenario: Proper mapping and fallback for null/empty values and COALESCE logic
    Given the SQL contains COALESCE, IS NULL, and CASE WHEN null/else logic
    When mapping to PySpark transformation
    Then corresponding PySpark logic uses DataFrame.na.fill, .when().otherwise(), and F.coalesce, matching the intended null/value semantics

  Scenario: Documentation and traceability requirements
    Given the PySpark code conversion is delivered
    Then all code sections are commented and documented to reference original SQL logic line/section
    And functions, constants, and parameter mappings are explained inline or in accompanying documentation to allow traceability

